# PLAN — etude-jqs: mirror fetched etude refs to a sibling namespace so fetch and prune are safe

## The defect

`etude-i19` REMOVED the `refs/etude/*` fetch refspec because `git fetch --prune`
was deleting unpushed run refs. That stopped the damage but left the cause:
`+refs/etude/*:refs/etude/*` maps origin's namespace onto the SAME local
namespace etude writes to, so git cannot distinguish "a run I produced and have
not pushed" from "a run that used to be on origin and is now gone". Prune cannot
be safe by construction. And with no fetch refspec at all, a bare `git fetch`
never brings the namespace down.

## The layout

```
refs/etude/runs/*                     MINE. Local, authoritative, NEVER pruned.
refs/etude/retros/*                   MINE.
refs/etude/evals/*                    MINE.
refs/etude-mirror/<remote>/runs/*     That remote's copy. Prunable, disposable.
refs/etude-mirror/<remote>/retros/*   Mirror.
refs/etude-mirror/<remote>/evals/*    Mirror.
```

Per remote, `etude init` registers three fetch refspecs and changes nothing else:

```
fetch = +refs/etude/runs/*:refs/etude-mirror/<name>/runs/*
fetch = +refs/etude/retros/*:refs/etude-mirror/<name>/retros/*
fetch = +refs/etude/evals/*:refs/etude-mirror/<name>/evals/*
```

A configured fetch refspec is SAFE again, because its destination is the mirror:
`git fetch --prune` can only ever delete from `refs/etude-mirror/*`, which is
disposable by definition. That restores the convenience i19 had to remove.

Forced (`+`) is correct here and only here: the mirror is a copy of the remote,
so it should always match the remote, and there is nothing of ours to lose in it.

## Why a SIBLING namespace, not `refs/etude/remotes/`

The bead originally specified nesting the mirror at
`refs/etude/remotes/<remote>/<kind>/*`. Four plan-gate rounds established that
THREE of the four blocking findings existed *only* because that nests the mirror
inside the namespace push refspecs span. Operator decision: use a sibling.

| hazard under nesting | under a sibling |
|---|---|
| i19's cleanup predicate removes any fetch refspec whose destination is inside `refs/etude/` — including the mirrored one this bead adds, so the second `etude init` self-destructs. Needs a permanent carve-out. | **Gone.** The mirror is outside `refs/etude/`; i19's predicate is used UNCHANGED. |
| The broad push `refs/etude/*:refs/etude/*` matches a nested mirror, so a push uploads this clone's mirror of origin back to origin. Needs a push-side migration on every already-initialised repo — which risks deleting user-owned push refspecs like `refs/etude/*:refs/archive/etude/*`. | **Gone.** No push refspec spanning `refs/etude/*` can match `refs/etude-mirror/*`. No migration, no deletion of user config. |
| That push hazard is repo-GLOBAL. `etude init --remote origin` can only migrate origin; a broad push left on `upstream` still matches `refs/etude/remotes/origin/*`, so `git push upstream` uploads origin's mirror. init must not edit another remote's config, so the only fix was refusing to install the mirrored fetch while ANY remote had a broad push — the headline feature silently not activating on multi-remote repos. | **Gone.** No remote's push refspec can match the mirror, so there is nothing global to coordinate. |

Cost, accepted: `git for-each-ref refs/etude/` no longer shows mirrors alongside
local refs; two prefixes are needed to see everything.

## What this change does NOT touch

Each of these needed changing under the nested layout and needs nothing now.
Stated explicitly so the shrunken diff is not mistaken for an incomplete one.

- **The push refspec** stays exactly as it is (`refs/etude/*:refs/etude/*`). It
  spans precisely the local namespaces, which is correct, and it cannot match the
  mirror. Narrowing it would be churn with no safety value, and would risk
  deleting a user's deliberate variant.
- **`etude sync`** needs no change. Both its explicit refspecs
  (`sync.go:94`, `sync.go:263`) span `refs/etude/*` and so cannot carry the
  mirror in either direction. Its fetch keeps materialising into the LOCAL
  namespace exactly as today, and stays non-forced (no `+`), which is why its
  non-fast-forward classification exists — a remote ref cannot overwrite a local
  unpushed run.
- **`gc`, `index`, `reindex`, `run`, `log`, `bench`, `liverun`** need no change.
  They read via `List("refs/etude/runs")` and friends, which cannot match
  `refs/etude-mirror/`.
- **i19's fetch-cleanup predicate** is used unchanged, carve-out free.

## Scope decision: the mirror is NOT a read source

Every reader keeps using the local namespace only. The mirror exists solely to
give a configured fetch refspec a safe destination.

The local run namespace is read from at least `cli/run.go:228,262`,
`cli/log.go:165`, `gc/gc.go:66,86,114,118,221,242`, `index/index.go:31`,
`bench/cohort.go:102`, `liverun/engine.go:272,429,441,535`, `liverun/gate.go:807`,
`liverun/gate_once.go:87`, `liverun/replay_forward.go:32`. Making the mirror
readable means touching all of them, each with its own precedence question, on
paths that resolve gate records.

Consequences, stated rather than discovered later:

- `git fetch` populates the mirror and `--prune` prunes it. Nothing else reads
  it yet: **it is inert storage in this commit.** The payoff is nonetheless
  immediate and independent of readability — prune becomes safe by construction,
  a fetch refspec can exist at all, and provenance becomes structural rather than
  inferred.
- Sharing behaviour is unchanged, because `etude sync` is unchanged.
- Divergence reporting over the two now-distinguishable namespaces is filed as
  **etude-eje**; mirror expiry (including after a remote is renamed or removed)
  as **etude-26i**.

## Migration ORDER — one rule survives, and it is i19's

Only one hazard remains, and it is layout-independent: a legacy hazardous FETCH
refspec (`+refs/etude/*:refs/etude/*`) surviving into any state prunes local run
refs. So it must be removed BEFORE the mirrored fetch refspecs are installed.
Removing the dangerous thing first also makes the earliest prefix the safest
state rather than the most exposed one.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCES: 1

Each step below is ONE `git config` invocation. Per-kind refspecs are enumerated
individually because they are written individually; collapsing them into one
conceptual step would make the residuals below false.

IRREVERSIBLE SEQUENCE mirror-fetch-migration: 4 steps
IRREVERSIBLE STEP mirror-fetch-migration.1: remove every fetch refspec on the
target remote whose destination lands in the local etude namespace.
IRREVERSIBLE STEP mirror-fetch-migration.2: install the mirrored fetch refspec
for the runs kind.
IRREVERSIBLE STEP mirror-fetch-migration.3: install the mirrored fetch refspec
for the retros kind.
IRREVERSIBLE STEP mirror-fetch-migration.4: install the mirrored fetch refspec
for the evals kind.

CRASH-PREFIX RESIDUALS:
CRASH-PREFIX mirror-fetch-migration.1: no etude fetch refspec of any kind is
CONFIGURED; push config untouched. Sharing still works via the unchanged push
refspec and `etude sync`.

The precise claim is **"no NEW fetch can be started with the legacy refspec after
this step"** — NOT "run refs are not prunable". A `git fetch --prune` that
started before this step read its refspecs at startup and still holds the legacy
one, so it can prune an unpushed local run after the removal completes. Git's
config lock orders writes, not readers that have already read; removing config
cannot reach into a running process.

The window is one-time (it exists during migration, not as an ongoing property)
and INHERITED rather than introduced: etude-i19 already removes this refspec and
has exactly the same race today. No ordering available to either bead closes it,
because the hazardous state lives in another process's memory.
CRASH-PREFIX mirror-fetch-migration.2: as .1, plus runs mirrored on fetch. A bare
fetch mirrors runs; retros and evals are simply not mirrored yet. No push refspec
can match the mirror, so it cannot leave this clone. Incomplete mirroring, no
data loss. Safe.
CRASH-PREFIX mirror-fetch-migration.3: as .2, plus retros. Safe.
CRASH-PREFIX mirror-fetch-migration.4: fully migrated — all three kinds mirrored
on fetch, no legacy fetch refspec remaining, push unchanged.

No ref is moved, rewritten or deleted at any point. Existing
`refs/etude/{runs,retros,evals}/*` stay exactly where they are and remain the
local authoritative refs. A repo that never re-runs `etude init` simply has no
fetch refspec — the post-i19 state, which is safe.

## Remote names

The mirror path interpolates the remote name, so it is validated by CONSTRUCTING
the path and checking it with git's own ref-format rules (`git check-ref-format`,
which `refstore.validateRef` already uses). A name that cannot produce a legal
ref is an error at init time, because a refspec that cannot work must not be
written. `prod:backup` is the motivating case: a colon is both illegal in a ref
name and the refspec's own `src:dst` separator, so it breaks at two levels.

A remote name containing `/` (e.g. `team/origin`) is **accepted**, not rejected.
It is a valid git remote name today, rejecting it would break a working
configuration, and git itself has exactly the same directory/file consideration
for `refs/remotes/team/origin/*`. This change does not invent a validation git
does not perform; a genuine D/F collision surfaces as git's own ref-store error.

**Known limitation, accepted deliberately: the mirror path is NOT injective for
slash-containing remote names.** Remote `team` holding `refs/etude/runs/runs/x`
and remote `team/runs` holding `refs/etude/runs/x` both construct
`refs/etude-mirror/team/runs/runs/x`. Constructed-ref validation cannot detect
this — it is cross-remote ALIASING, and both paths are perfectly legal refs — so
`git fetch --prune team/runs` could delete a mirror entry belonging to `team`.

So the provenance claim splits:

- **LOCAL vs MIRROR provenance IS structural.** `refs/etude/<kind>/*` versus
  `refs/etude-mirror/…` is unambiguous, and that is the distinction this bead
  exists to create — the one that makes prune safe.
- **WHICH-REMOTE provenance is NOT injective** when a remote name contains `/`.

Bounded impact: a collision can only corrupt the MIRROR, which is disposable by
definition and re-derived by the next fetch. No local authoritative ref is
reachable from it and no push refspec matches it, so nothing incorrect can be
uploaded. It is inherited from git's own `refs/remotes/<name>/…` layout, which
aliases identically — the same precedent that justifies accepting `team/origin`.
Filed as **etude-cng** with the injective fix (percent-escaping `/` in the remote
component) and its costs recorded, so the choice is auditable rather than silent.

